  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942972/assets/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(-x)=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(-x+8\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(-x) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(-x+8\\pi )+7 & =6(-\\frac{5}{3})+7 \\\\  & =-10+7 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
